        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place the yoghurt in a medium bowl. Add the grated garlic and stir to combine."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the chopped mint, dill and raisins to the yoghurt. Stir gently until evenly distributed."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the rose water and stir through. Taste and adjust. The dip should be cool, herby and very lightly floral."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cover with cling film and refrigerate for at least 30 minutes. The resting time allows the herbs to soften and the garlic to mellow."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Transfer to a shallow bowl to serve. Drizzle with olive oil and add a few extra mint leaves or dried rose petals if you have them. Serve cold alongside any Persian meal."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Rose water flavour is overpowering",
              "solution": "Rose water varies in strength by brand. Start with half a teaspoon and add more after tasting. It should be a background note, not the dominant flavour."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Yoghurt is too runny",
              "solution": "Use a full-fat strained yoghurt. If the yoghurt is thin, strain it through a muslin cloth in the fridge for 30 minutes first to thicken it."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Garlic flavour is too sharp",
              "solution": "Grate the garlic very finely so it disperses evenly and is less harsh. One clove is enough. Making the dip ahead gives the raw garlic time to mellow in the yoghurt."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Mast-o Khiar appears on every Persian table as one of a series of small cold side dishes called meze. It cools down spiced rice and rich stews. The addition of raisins and rose water is specifically Persian and sets it apart from similar yoghurt dips found elsewhere in the Middle East. It is one of the first things children in Iran learn to make."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
